---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Newsletter from '../components/Newsletter.astro';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="Sid Thesis brings an engineering perspective to questions about business, financial markets, and capital allocation."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">About Sid Thesis</p>
      <h1>
        Different disciplines.
        <br />
        <em>Deeper questions.</em>
      </h1>
      <p>
        An independent publication about technology, business, and capital—and
        the connections between them.
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="about-grid">
      <aside>
        <div class="author-monogram" aria-hidden="true">
          S<span>.</span>
        </div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Sid / Author</p>
        <p class="muted">
          Software engineer.
          <br />
          Independent researcher.
        </p>
      </aside>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>A perspective from building</h2>
        <p>
          I’m Sid, a software engineer with a background in quantitative
          finance, financial markets, distributed systems, payments, and
          entrepreneurship.
        </p>
        <p>
          Engineering draws my attention to how systems work: their constraints,
          tradeoffs, and points of failure. Finance adds another set of
          questions: how incentives shape behavior, how risk is priced, and who
          captures the value.
        </p>
        <p>
          Sid Thesis is a place to bring these perspectives together through
          independent, long-form research.
        </p>
        <h2>Following the connections</h2>
        <p>
          A technical capability can change the cost of doing business. A
          business model can reshape a market. A shift in market structure can
          change where capital flows. Understanding one layer often requires
          looking closely at the others.
        </p>
        <p>
          The research here is intended for readers who want to look beneath the
          headline: investors, founders, engineers, and anyone interested in the
          economics of technological change.
        </p>
        <h2>A standard for the work</h2>
        <p>
          The aim is to make the reasoning inspectable: distinguish facts from
          assumptions, link to original sources, state uncertainties, and revise
          conclusions when the evidence changes.
        </p>
        <p>
          Upcoming work is labeled as such. A research question is a starting
          point, not a claim that the answer is already known.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/research/">
          Explore the research ↗
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Newsletter />
  </div>
</Base>
